@kb1slnlabs/dsh-linear README · Windows quick start (DSH Desktop)：2. **Install the plugin into the Desktop profile** with the DSH CLI bundled inside
   the Desktop installation (verified path — this is the one to use for Desktop;
   do not rely on a `dsh` on your PATH, which may belong to a different install):

```powershell
   & "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Programs\DeepSeek Harness\resources\runtime\cli\bin\dsh.cmd" plugin --profile desktop add https://github.com/KB1SLN-Labs/dsh-linear/releases/download/v0.2.5/kb1slnlabs-dsh-linear.tgz
   ```

@kb1slnlabs/dsh-linear README · https://github.com/KB1SLN-Labs/dsh-linear/releases/download/v0.2.5/kb1slnlabs-dsh-linear.tgz：To add it as a plain dependency instead of a link, use `file:<absolute-path>` or a
packaged tarball (`npm pack`, then `dsh plugin --profile web add file:<path-to-tgz>`) —
that is how the scratch profile used by [Testing](#testing) was built.

@kb1slnlabs/dsh-linear README · How identifiers are resolved (resolve-then-mutate)：- a team key/name → `teams(filter: { key: { eq: … } })` → `teamId`
- a state name → `workflowStates(filter: { team: { id: { eq: <teamId> } }, name: { eq: … } })` → `stateId`
- a project name → `projects(filter: { name: { eq: … } })` → the mutation's top-level `id`
